<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dionysius I of Syracuse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 432–367 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Dionysius the Elder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dionysius I was tyrant of Syracuse from 405 to 367 BCE and the most powerful Greek ruler in the west of his time. He fought repeated wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, besieging and destroying Motya in 397 BCE with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">siege towers</a> and the newly invented catapult. He also conquered or destroyed Greek cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catana</a> and extended his power into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse.jpg" alt="Dionysius I of Syracuse" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Max Karl Baldamus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dionisio_I_(Ritratto).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
